1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,301
This is CaliDoc, by Mohammed Hamdan and Mohamed
Cheriet, from the Synchromedia

2
00:00:06,301 --> 00:00:08,430
Laboratory at ÉTS Montréal.

3
00:00:09,091 --> 00:00:14,799
When a document extractor comes with a conformal
guarantee, what exactly is that

4
00:00:14,799 --> 00:00:15,941
guarantee about?

5
00:00:16,522 --> 00:00:22,402
We show it can be about the wrong object, and
that fixing the object also lets us

6
00:00:22,402 --> 00:00:23,999
audit the annotations.

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:31,525
Extraction feeds expense systems, records and
compliance checks, and those systems

8
00:00:31,525 --> 00:00:36,081
consume complete fields: a company
name, an address, a total.

9
00:00:36,743 --> 00:00:40,482
A field is right only if every word
in its span is right.

10
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:45,137
One wrong word in an eleven-word
address is a wrong address.

11
00:00:45,497 --> 00:00:51,845
Conformal prediction for key information
extraction has so far certified tokens, or

12
00:00:51,845 --> 00:00:54,599
one representative token per entity.

13
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:58,419
Does that proxy tell us anything
about fields?

14
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:07,856
On SROIE, a LayoutLMv3-Large tagger reaches an
entity F1 of about 94.5, so the

15
00:01:07,856 --> 00:01:09,445
extractor is good.

16
00:01:09,839 --> 00:01:16,373
Standard token-level conformal prediction, at a
90% target, gives 89.8% token

17
00:01:16,373 --> 00:01:17,049
coverage.

18
00:01:17,385 --> 00:01:20,067
By the usual criterion,
calibration worked.

19
00:01:20,358 --> 00:01:21,937
Now count complete fields.

20
00:01:22,482 --> 00:01:27,335
Of 420 gold field spans, not one has all
of its tokens covered.

21
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:28,519
Zero.

22
00:01:29,216 --> 00:01:35,509
The reason: a single marginal threshold is sized
for the O class, 84% of tokens.

23
00:01:35,799 --> 00:01:41,964
Six of nine label classes get zero coverage,
including all four field-initial B

24
00:01:41,964 --> 00:01:45,632
classes, and every field begins
with a B token.

25
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:50,903
A token-level guarantee was never a
statement about fields.

26
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:58,513
Here is one real SROIE test receipt, scored by
the released checkpoint, shown as

27
00:01:58,513 --> 00:02:02,319
structure because the dataset
licence is unclear.

28
00:02:02,981 --> 00:02:06,325
Each dot is the score of a
field's worst word.

29
00:02:06,812 --> 00:02:13,173
The red line is the token-marginal threshold:
every field's worst word lies beyond

30
00:02:13,173 --> 00:02:15,810
it, so all four fields are missed.

31
00:02:16,472 --> 00:02:22,316
The teal ticks are CaliDoc's field-level
thresholds, and all four fields fall inside

32
00:02:22,316 --> 00:02:22,660
them.

33
00:02:23,322 --> 00:02:25,644
The extractor is unchanged.

34
00:02:26,004 --> 00:02:32,331
What changes is the object we calibrate: the
complete field, with the document as

35
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:34,050
the exchangeable unit.

36
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:38,480
CaliDoc sits on top of a fixed extractor.

37
00:02:39,142 --> 00:02:45,131
Word probabilities are temperature-scaled on a
held-out early-stopping split, and

38
00:02:45,131 --> 00:02:48,384
each field span is scored by
its worst word.

39
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:54,965
One calibrated threshold then serves two paths:
a calibration path that returns

40
00:02:54,965 --> 00:03:00,972
calibrated candidate sets for every extracted
field, and an audit path that asks

41
00:03:00,972 --> 00:03:03,825
where inside a field the escapes land.

42
00:03:04,486 --> 00:03:06,750
Nothing is retrained.

43
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:09,752
Two design decisions.

44
00:03:10,089 --> 00:03:11,598
First, the score.

45
00:03:12,051 --> 00:03:17,813
A span is fully covered at a threshold lambda
exactly when its worst word is below

46
00:03:17,813 --> 00:03:23,505
lambda, so the maximum word score turns full-span
coverage into a one-dimensional

47
00:03:23,505 --> 00:03:24,067
problem.

48
00:03:24,613 --> 00:03:28,340
A mean or a quantile would certify
a different event.

49
00:03:28,827 --> 00:03:30,232
Second, the unit.

50
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:36,877
Spans on one page are not exchangeable, because
one misaligned OCR line corrupts

51
00:03:36,877 --> 00:03:38,092
several of them.

52
00:03:38,754 --> 00:03:44,258
So the document is the exchangeable unit, and its
loss is the fraction of its gold

53
00:03:44,258 --> 00:03:45,802
fields that are missed.

54
00:03:46,463 --> 00:03:52,454
Conformal risk control picks the smallest lambda
whose corrected calibration risk is

55
00:03:52,454 --> 00:03:53,453
at most alpha.

56
00:03:54,196 --> 00:04:00,265
The theorem: on a fresh exchangeable document
with at least one gold field, the

57
00:04:00,265 --> 00:04:04,644
expected fraction of missed gold
fields is at most alpha.

58
00:04:05,062 --> 00:04:06,931
That is an expectation.

59
00:04:07,349 --> 00:04:13,272
It is not a promise about any single document,
nor about each field type, and the

60
00:04:13,272 --> 00:04:16,417
coverage numbers that follow
are empirical.

61
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:23,787
Running the same risk control per field type gives
each type its own threshold, and

62
00:04:23,787 --> 00:04:29,825
the deployed candidate-label rule makes the
calibrated and deployed events coincide.

63
00:04:30,486 --> 00:04:35,425
Types with fewer than twenty-five calibration
spans fall back to the pooled

64
00:04:35,425 --> 00:04:36,083
threshold.

65
00:04:36,663 --> 00:04:42,213
On SROIE all four types are supported;
on CORD most are not.

66
00:04:42,875 --> 00:04:48,401
Formally, the pooled bound holds and each
supported type has its own bound.

67
00:04:48,982 --> 00:04:54,823
Fallback types inherit only the pooled bound,
and aggregate coverage under this

68
00:04:54,823 --> 00:04:56,597
mixed rule is empirical.

69
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,177
At the 90% target, the token-marginal threshold
leaves SROIE with a realized

70
00:05:04,177 --> 00:05:10,841
field-miss risk of 1.000: every field
missed on every document.

71
00:05:11,491 --> 00:05:19,576
Field-conditional SpanCRC brings it to 0.091,
against the 0.10 bound on its

72
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:20,442
expectation.

73
00:05:21,081 --> 00:05:28,826
Empirically, full-span coverage rises from zero to
0.909 and span CovGap falls from

74
00:05:28,826 --> 00:05:33,295
90 points to 2.1, for sets about
ten percent larger.

75
00:05:33,782 --> 00:05:39,797
On WildReceipt and FUNSD, where the marginal
threshold already worked, the method

76
00:05:39,797 --> 00:05:42,525
leaves results roughly where they were.

77
00:05:43,012 --> 00:05:47,877
It helps in proportion to how badly the
marginal threshold fails.

78
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:51,347
CORD is our weakest case.

79
00:05:51,765 --> 00:05:59,894
Most field types fall back, so span CovGap
improves only from 42.3 to 33.3 points,

80
00:05:59,894 --> 00:06:05,813
while average set size grows
from 0.90 to 2.92.

81
00:06:06,393 --> 00:06:13,896
A level-calibrated interpolation brings set size
down to 1.12, but it keeps only the

82
00:06:13,896 --> 00:06:18,305
pooled document-level bound, not a
field-conditional one.

83
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,068
The same threshold supports an audit.

84
00:06:22,382 --> 00:06:26,376
A word escapes when its gold tag scores
beyond the threshold.

85
00:06:27,038 --> 00:06:32,781
In spans where some words escape but not all, we
ask whether escapes concentrate on

86
00:06:32,781 --> 00:06:37,486
the two boundary words, the signature of
alignment and BIO errors.

87
00:06:38,148 --> 00:06:43,948
The null is a conditional randomization test: given
each span's length and number of

88
00:06:43,948 --> 00:06:49,747
escapes, escaped positions are uniform within the
span and independent across spans.

89
00:06:50,327 --> 00:06:52,417
Holm corrects across field types.

90
00:06:52,997 --> 00:06:59,687
Two caveats: cross-span independence is an
assumption, so every finding also needs a

91
00:06:59,687 --> 00:07:06,298
document-blocked bootstrap interval to agree; and
spans of two words or fewer carry

92
00:07:06,298 --> 00:07:08,369
no positional information.

93
00:07:09,770 --> 00:07:17,798
At familywise level 0.10, the test rejects two of
SROIE's fields: ADDRESS, with edge

94
00:07:17,798 --> 00:07:28,584
enrichment 4.07, and COMPANY, 1.80, both with a
Holm-adjusted p of 0.0008 and

95
00:07:28,584 --> 00:07:31,341
bootstrap intervals excluding one.

96
00:07:31,922 --> 00:07:36,438
None of sixteen eligible fields on the
clean benchmarks is rejected.

97
00:07:36,926 --> 00:07:38,946
Then the falsification test.

98
00:07:39,526 --> 00:07:45,430
We re-derived SROIE's labels independently from
the official Task 3 field strings,

99
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:47,014
as a separate lineage.

100
00:07:47,676 --> 00:07:52,541
ADDRESS enrichment falls to 0.79 and
nothing is rejected.

101
00:07:52,958 --> 00:07:59,316
Calibration did not repair the data; an
independent re-alignment did, and the audit

102
00:07:59,316 --> 00:08:00,389
registered it.

103
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:07,858
The closest prior work, Rombach and Mehdiyev,
brought split conformal prediction to

104
00:08:07,858 --> 00:08:13,781
key information extraction, certifying a
representative first token with marginal

105
00:08:13,781 --> 00:08:16,047
coverage over entity instances.

106
00:08:16,709 --> 00:08:20,923
We certify the complete field as a
document-level risk.

107
00:08:21,585 --> 00:08:28,798
At their operating point, the two constructions are
stated side by side, not ranked,

108
00:08:28,798 --> 00:08:33,091
because backbone, score, schema
and splits differ.

109
00:08:34,490 --> 00:08:36,417
The limitations are in the paper.

110
00:08:36,731 --> 00:08:39,947
The guarantee is a risk, not a coverage.

111
00:08:40,504 --> 00:08:44,858
Field-conditional calibration is
support-limited, as CORD shows.

112
00:08:45,415 --> 00:08:49,130
The audit is blind on spans of two
words or fewer.

113
00:08:49,699 --> 00:08:52,428
Its null assumes cross-span independence.

114
00:08:52,741 --> 00:08:56,305
The real-data positive rests on
SROIE alone.

115
00:08:56,967 --> 00:09:03,422
And the scope is four English receipt and form
corpora, with one backbone family.

116
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:06,039
Three takeaways.

117
00:09:06,457 --> 00:09:10,520
Token-level reliability is not
complete-field reliability.

118
00:09:11,008 --> 00:09:17,025
Make the delivered field the unit: document-level
risk control bounds the expected

119
00:09:17,025 --> 00:09:20,401
fraction of missed fields,
without retraining.

120
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,534
And use the same calibration to
audit annotations.

121
00:09:25,021 --> 00:09:30,292
Code, checkpoints, the supplement and a
Colab demo are linked here.

122
00:09:30,652 --> 00:09:34,286
This work was supported by an
N-SERC Discovery Grant.

123
00:09:35,029 --> 00:09:38,442
Report reliability for the object
you actually deliver.

124
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:39,696
Thank you.

